=== Domestic War and emigration (1932–1937) ===

The declaration of the [[Socialist Republic of Burawa]] in 1932 and the subsequent [[Burawi Domestic War]] (1932–1935) triggered a wave of emigration rather than mass civilian casualties. The displaced liberal and landowning classes, segments of the former officer corps, and populations in the peripheral regions that departed under the [[Act of Self-Determination]] together constituted the majority of population losses during this period. Fighting was concentrated in the peripheries of the country and did not produce the scale of civilian death associated with the subsequent Great Adonian War.

The principal destinations for emigrants were [[Galwa]], [[Orma]], and [[Volisania]]. The Burawi diaspora in these countries remained significant throughout the twentieth century. Some returned during the post-war reconstruction period; others established permanent communities abroad.

=== Great Adonian War (1935–1948) ===

The [[Great Adonian War]] inflicted far greater physical destruction than the Domestic War. The [[Volisania|Volisan]] bombing campaign devastated the southern coast, with [[Iskhal]] suffering particularly heavy damage. The population declined from approximately 28.4 million in 1937 to 21 million by 1948, a loss of approximately 7.4 million persons through military casualties, civilian deaths from bombardment, reduced birth rates, and continued emigration.

The demographic effects were severe. The birth rate fell from 23 per 1,000 to approximately 12 per 1,000 at the height of the conflict. The gender ratio declined from 95 men per 100 women in 1937 to approximately 76 men per 100 women by 1945, reflecting the disproportionate impact of military casualties on the male population. The resulting cohort gaps remained visible in the population structure for decades.

=== Post-war malaise (1948–1962) ===

The end of the Great Adonian War did not produce an immediate demographic recovery. The period from 1948 to 1962 was characterised by economic stagnation, political uncertainty, and a pervasive societal malaise that suppressed population growth. The death of [[Manaa Kadar]] in 1945 left a leadership vacuum at the centre of the republic; the physical devastation of the southern coast, particularly [[Iskhal]], required reconstruction on a scale that exceeded the capacity of the post-war economy; and the political direction of the state remained contested among competing factions within the [[Kadarist Party]] and its successors.

The demographic consequences were twofold. The birth rate, which had collapsed during the war, recovered only slowly, remaining well below pre-war levels throughout the 1950s. Emigration also continued during this period, as economic conditions in Burawa compared unfavourably with those in neighbouring countries that had avoided the worst of the war. The population grew from 21 million in 1948 to 35 million by 1962, a rate sustained principally by returning diaspora and modest immigration rather than natural increase during the early years, with natural increase accelerating only in the late 1950s as reconstruction progressed.

The malaise ended with the [[1962 Crisis]], which resolved the political uncertainty that had constrained the republic since Kadar's death and inaugurated a period of economic and demographic expansion.

=== Baby boom (1962–1985) ===

The [[Burawi baby boom]] began in 1962, following the resolution of the [[1962 Crisis]] and a period of renewed economic confidence. Between 1962 and 1985, the population more than doubled from 35 million to 76 million, driven by annual birth rates of 30–35 per 1,000 and declining mortality. The boom generation, now aged 41–64, constitutes the largest share of the current working-age population and the dominant demographic cohort in Burawi society.

The end of the baby boom in 1985 initiated a gradual decline in birth rates that has continued to the present. The fertility rate has fallen to approximately 1.62 children per woman, well below the replacement rate of 2.1. The gradual rather than abrupt nature of this decline distinguishes Burawa from countries that experienced sharp demographic transitions.

