The overall gender ratio of 112 men per 100 women conceals a significant divergence between the citizen and non-citizen populations. Among citizens, women outnumber men, reflecting the lingering demographic effects of the war generation and the longer life expectancy of women. Among non-citizens, men outnumber women by a substantial margin, as the majority of immigrant workers are employed in male-dominated industries such as mining, heavy industry, and construction.

=== Dependency ratio ===

The {{w|dependency ratio}} — the ratio of persons aged 0–14 and 65+ to those aged 15–64 — is approximately 43 per 100 working-age persons (12% youth + 18% elderly = 30% dependent, 70% working-age). The ratio has increased steadily since 2000 as the baby boom generation has begun to enter retirement, and is projected to rise further over the coming decades. This trend was a principal motivation for the expansion of immigration programmes in the 1990s and 2000s, intended to sustain the working-age population and fund the pension system.

== Citizenship and immigration ==

=== Citizen and non-citizen population ===

Approximately 78% of the population (99.5 million persons) are Burawi citizens, while 22% (28 million persons) are non-citizens. The non-citizen population comprises permanent residents on the ten-year [[Burawi citizenship|citizenship pathway]], temporary workers under the [[Temporary Worker Program Act (Burawa)|Temporary Worker Program Act]] of 2008, and other foreign residents.

