=== Emigration and the Burawi diaspora ===

The [[Burawi Domestic War]] (1932–1935) produced the largest emigration event in Burawi history. The displaced landowning and liberal classes, former military officers, and populations opposed to the [[Socialist Republic of Burawa|socialist republic]] departed in large numbers, principally to [[Galwa]], [[Orma]], and [[Volisania]]. This diaspora remained politically and culturally significant throughout the twentieth century. A portion returned during the post-war reconstruction period, though substantial Burawi communities persist in all three countries.

=== Immigration ===

Burawa has experienced continuous immigration throughout its modern history, though the scale and character of immigration have varied by period.

Prior to the wars, non-citizens comprised approximately 12% of the population, primarily merchants and workers from neighbouring [[Southwestern Kaftia|Southwestern Kaftian]] countries. During the wars, most non-citizens departed, and the non-citizen share fell to 4% by 1945.

Post-war reconstruction attracted significant immigration, and the non-citizen population grew steadily through the 1950s. During the 1950s and 1960s, Burawa's socialist alliances produced a distinctive pattern of exchange immigration: citizens of [[Daras]] and [[Balisca]], allied socialist states, settled in Burawa as part of diplomatic and educational exchange programmes. These communities concentrated in [[Kankadadka]] and [[Samara]], the administrative and military capitals. Reciprocally, Burawi communities were established in Daras and Balisca during this period.

The most transformative period of immigration began in the 1990s, driven by Burawa's economic growth and labour demands in heavy industry, mining, and construction. The largest sources of immigration are [[Enfolo]] and [[Dakare]]. Immigrant workers have settled principally in the [[Lake Haro]] and [[Buran River Valley]] regions, where industrial employment is concentrated, and in the [[Dimar]] region, which has developed significant ethnic diversity.

The [[Temporary Worker Program Act (Burawa)|Temporary Worker Program Act]] of 2008 formalised a system of renewable short-term contracts (six months to three years) for industries experiencing labour shortages. The number of temporary workers peaked at 2.4 million in 2015 and stood at approximately 1.5 million in 2026. Immigrants who remain on a permanent basis may apply for citizenship through the ten-year work-to-citizenship pathway, which requires continuous employment and sponsorship by a Burawi company for the full ten-year period.

