Burawa has a literacy rate of 96.2% among the adult population. University attendance is very high, reflecting the country's developed status and comprehensive public education system.

== Health ==

Life expectancy at birth is 82.4 years, reflecting the country's advanced healthcare system and high standard of living. The death rate is approximately 5.7 per 1,000 persons.

The leading causes of death are {{w|cardiovascular disease}}, {{w|cancer}}, and {{w|cerebrovascular disease}}, consistent with the epidemiological profile of an aging developed nation. {{w|Road traffic injuries}} and {{w|diabetes mellitus}} are also significant causes of mortality, particularly in the working-age population.

== Households and family ==

{{main|Housing in Burawa}}

All housing in Burawa, with the exception of property in [[Mazar]], is owned by the central government and leased to residents through the [[Burawi Housing System]]. Housing is a constitutional right, and all legal residents over the age of 18 are entitled to apply for a residence. A single-occupancy household is guaranteed a minimum of 25 m², with an additional 28 m² per additional household member, up to seven persons.

Households are defined as persons who are legally related to each other, including siblings, parents, grandparents, children, [[Domestic Partnerships in Burawa|domestic partners]], and spouses. The [[Domestic Partnership Act of 1996 (Burawa)|Domestic Partnership Act of 1996]] devolved the definition of legal domestic partnership to localities; some urban administrations in the 2010s extended recognition to multi-adult households, permitting friend groups to apply for larger cohabited spaces.

Subletting, renting, and the private exchange of property are prohibited by law. Property agencies operate in a legal grey area, charging commissions for advisory services related to the identification and claiming of desirable housing stock.

