Burawa is the fifth-largest economy on [[Adonia]] by purchasing power parity. The country has a relatively low level of income inequality, with a Gini coefficient of 24.5, and is classified in the very high human development category.

== Population projections ==

The aging of the baby boom generation (born 1962–1985) represents the principal demographic challenge facing Burawa over the coming decades. As this cohort enters retirement, the dependency ratio is projected to rise significantly, placing increased pressure on the pension system, healthcare infrastructure, and working-age labour supply. The expansion of immigration programmes since the 1990s, including the [[Temporary Worker Program Act (Burawa)|Temporary Worker Program Act]] of 2008, was in part a policy response to this projected demographic shift, intended to sustain the working-age population as the citizen birth rate remained below replacement level.

